		"nodes": {
			"1": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "SHUFFLE\nThe Shuffle node takes an array and shuffles it’s channels. This node is a great way to randomize your patch when using instances. To learn more about instancing, please consult the manual.\n\nPatch Explained -\n\n• The two Linear nodes give us an array with 10 channels running from -1 to 1.\n\n• The Shuffle node shuffles the channels before passing it into the Gate. You can press the ‘reshuffle’ button to randomize the output again.\n\n•  The shuffled and unshuffled arrays both go into a Gate. You can switch between the two by using the Bool. This is purely for demonstration purposes to show you the difference.\n\n• The output of the gate modulates the phase offset of the Sine Oscillator. This means that each instance of the ten sines is offset a bit. This causes the wave motion of the rectangles as the sine modulates the Y-position of the Transform node.\n\n• The X of the Transform is modulated by another Linear node to make sure that the rectangles are distributed across the screen.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 421.301422119140625,
					"width": 810.9566650390625,
					"x": -38.81798171997070312,
